format:skit:
A skit is a post with a short AI-made parody video: one or two of the card's `people` staged in an obviously impossible, absurd, expensive-looking scene, three or four shots (each 3 to 6 seconds), made as one take: one film of up to 20 seconds, hard cuts, the same face, the same set and one continuous score. The premise is what the figure did or claimed on the card, pushed to an impossible end ("X did Y, so X now does Z"). The figure drives every shot and the impossible thing is in frame in every shot. First shot sets up the real thing so plainly that someone with the sound off gets it; second escalates; last is the payoff and ends on a held still image. Say once, as data, what holds across the film: `look` (one line a figure: hair, glasses, wardrobe, prop), `setting` (one line: the place, its light, the lens, the film look), `score` (one line: the one piece of music under it all), `teeny` (one line: what Teeny does in the last shot. Teeny is a bystander with a verdict: its own small thing, apart from everyone and noticed by nobody, that says what to think of the whole room, such as eating the cake out of the bin, facing the camera and shrugging, or walking out. Write only Teeny and objects in it: no person, no pronoun for one, nothing Teeny looks at, touches, hands over or talks to, no writing and no money. Teeny is only ever in `teeny`, never in a shot). Each shot then says only what changes: the framing, the figure's full name and the one thing they do, what reacts, at most one short spoken line (about six words) and its sound. No sign, screen or banner with writing on it. The tone of every skit: defiant (nobody is sorry or embarrassed; the figure does it at the people watching, on purpose, and the contempt points up, at the board, the regulator, the rule), extreme (the idea goes all the way to its limit, never half), classy (champagne, velvet, chandeliers, tailoring: the more expensive it looks, the ruder it reads) and horny for the thing the story is about (the chip, the money, the merger). That thing is the shameless one: it flirts, it is dressed for it, and it gets the heart eyes and the kiss. The lust is never for a person and nothing sexual is shown about a real person. And it makes sense: each shot is caused by the one before it, starting from the fact on the card. The post's own words say what happened in a clause and frame the video as your parody. The video model is sent one script and the guard refuses one over 1000 characters, so the names, looks, setting, score and every shot's prompt come to at most 436 characters with one figure (408 with two, 380 with three).

A skit's mechanics: it carries an 'AI parody' label the whole way through, so the post's words never call it real, leaked or caught on camera; no logo or brand shown endorsing anything; no @-mentions and no links.

